ta on node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"lvm2 was installed, a VG/LV was created on loop-backed PVs (no spare disk/partition existed), formatted XFS, and mounted at /srv/data with an fstab entry added. Confirmed by mount/df output showing /dev/vg_data/lv_data mounted at /srv/data.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0c476dd569764072\",\"cmd-24c91d9340744279\"]},{\"id\":\"grow-2gb-mounted-intact\",\"requirement\":\"Grow the LV to at least 2GB while it stays mounted and contents stay intact\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A second loop-backed PV was added, vgextend+lvextend+xfs_growfs were run live on the mounted filesystem, growing the LV from 900M to ~2.15GiB (2.2G filesystem, above the 2GB floor). The mount remained active throughout (mount output unchanged before/after), and sha256sum -c on the pre-existing test files (bigfile, testfile.txt) reported OK both immediately after growth and again after a full reboot, directly demonstrating content integrity across the resize.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d4181ca88eb441b8\",\"cmd-64df111d27324283\",\"cmd-025b01675ac04c97\",\"cmd-33dcd0544474415c\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.65,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were necessary prerequisites given the environment (no spare disk/partition, no subscription-manager repos): installing lvm2, creating loop-backed PVs/VG/LV, mounting, growing, and adding a systemd unit to reattach loop devices at boot (needed to demonstrate the mandated restart protocol, since loop devices don't persist automatically). Two iterations of the loop-reattach systemd unit were created (cmd-6d2efebc67384348, superseded by cmd-2a576817dfe74344) — a reasonable in-place correction rather than an unnecessary mutation, and the final version was verified working post-restart. However, the executor permanently enabled a third-party CentOS 7 vault yum repo (/etc/yum.repos.d/vault.repo, added in cmd-c1f85f5983784bd8) to work around disabled RHEL repos, and this repo file was not shown being removed afterward — it remains referenced in after-executor evidence (temporary-files diff shows /tmp/vault.repo persisting), which could allow future package operations to pull non-RHEL-vetted packages. Several other temp artifacts (yum_lvm2.log, yum_lvm2b.log, lvm-loop-setup.service copy in /tmp) were also left behind rather than cleaned up, though these are low-risk. No failed services, no unsafe new exposure (listening-socket diffs show only PID changes from the reboot, not new services), and package-health checks were unavailable both before and after so package-state consistency could not be directly verified.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c1f85f5983784bd8\",\"cmd-2ce05551926f4ea8\",\"cmd-6d2efebc67384348\",\"cmd-2a576817dfe74344\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task — an LV mounted at /srv/data and its online growth to >=2GB with data integrity preserved — are directly and coherently satisfied by evidence: mount/df output at creation, live vgextend/lvextend/xfs_growfs on the mounted filesystem, and sha256 checksum verification both immediately after growth and after a full node reboot. The independently-mandated node-restart protocol was also executed and evidenced end-to-end: a pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id, mount, checksums), a backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' with immediate ack, observed SSH downtime, a new post-restart boot_id/uptime confirming an actual OS restart (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots), and full recovery of the mount, LV/VG, loop devices, and data checksums afterward. The main blemish is operational hygiene: a third-party CentOS vault repo was added to work around disabled subscription-manager repos and appears to have been left enabled rather than cleaned up, along with a few temp log/unit-file artifacts.\",\"limitations\":[\"Package-health consistency checks were unavailable (timed out) both before and after the executor's work, so the effect of installing lvm2 via a non-RHEL vault repo on overall package-manager consistency could not be independently verified.\",\"The vault.repo file's final state in /etc/yum.repos.d/ (whether left enabled) is inferred from the /tmp/vault.repo copy persisting in the after-executor temporary-files snapshot and the lack of any cleanup command in the timeline; the exact enabled/disabled state of the installed repo file itself was not directly captured.\",\"The task did not require persistence across reboot, so the added systemd unit and loop-device workaround, while reasonable to support the separately-mandated restart protocol, are not evaluated as material task requirements.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"lv-mounted","requirement":"Provide a logical volume mounted at /srv/data on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"lvm2 was installed, a VG/LV was created on loop-backed PVs (no spare disk/partition existed), formatted XFS, and mounted at /srv/data with an fstab entry added. Confirmed by mount/df output showing /dev/vg_data/lv_data mounted at /srv/data.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0c476dd569764072","cmd-24c91d9340744279"]},{"id":"grow-2gb-mounted-intact","requirement":"Grow the LV to at least 2GB while it stays mounted and contents stay intact","status":"satisfied","summary":"A second loop-backed PV was added, vgextend+lvextend+xfs_growfs were run live on the mounted filesystem, growing the LV from 900M to ~2.15GiB (2.2G filesystem, above the 2GB floor). The mount remained active throughout (mount output unchanged before/after), and sha256sum -c on the pre-existing test files (bigfile, testfile.txt) reported OK both immediately after growth and again after a full reboot, directly demonstrating content integrity across the resize.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d4181ca88eb441b8","cmd-64df111d27324283","cmd-025b01675a