mount/df output at creation, live vgextend/lvextend/xfs_growfs on the mounted filesystem, and sha256 checksum verification both immediately after growth and after a full node reboot. The independently-mandated node-restart protocol was also executed and evidenced end-to-end: a pre-restart healthy baseline (boot_id, mount, checksums), a backgrounded 'shutdown -r now' with immediate ack, observed SSH downtime, a new post-restart boot_id/uptime confirming an actual OS restart (cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots), and full recovery of the mount, LV/VG, loop devices, and data checksums afterward. The main blemish is operational hygiene: a third-party CentOS vault repo was added to work around disabled subscription-manager repos and appears to have been left enabled rather than cleaned up, along with a few temp log/unit-file artifacts.","limitations":["Package-health consistency checks were unavailable (timed out) both before and after the executor's work, so the effect of installing lvm2 via a non-RHEL vault repo on overall package-manager consistency could not be independently verified.","The vault.repo file's final state in /etc/yum.repos.d/ (whether left enabled) is inferred from the /tmp/vault.repo copy persisting in the after-executor temporary-files snapshot and the lack of any cleanup command in the timeline; the exact enabled/disabled state of the installed repo file itself was not directly captured.","The task did not require persistence across reboot, so the added systemd unit and loop-device workaround, while reasonable to support the separately-mandated restart protocol, are not evaluated as material task requirements."]},"ttft_ms":22590,"type":"result","duration_ms":56497,"uuid":"78edfb54-c4d8-4283-b207-c8b7ff6c4270","ttft_stream_ms":1931,"time_to_request_ms":46,"first_content_frame_ms":2727,"queued_turn_count":0}
